{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are generally below realized prices by ~1–3 EUR for most of the horizon, especially during uptrends (model underestimates bullish drift).",
    "Trend/level lag: when the market steps up (rapid rally), the forecast adjusts too slowly and stays anchored to the prior 30d mean; apply an upward level shift early in the horizon when recent returns are positive.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot: beyond ~day 20–30 the model often projects continued acceleration (sometimes to 70+), while realized prices flatten or revert; dampen slope/volatility and pull long-horizon paths back toward a medium-term mean.",
    "Turning-point miss: after peaks the model remains too high (fails to capture drawdowns), implying insufficient mean reversion; introduce stronger reversion after large positive moves and when spot is extended above recent average.",
    "Volatility misallocation: model underestimates near-term volatility during rallies (misses sharp upside days) but overstates volatility in the tail (excessive dispersion); increase short-horizon responsiveness and reduce far-horizon variance."
  ]
}
